on more  leverage if you will as it tries to  negotiate a new deal with ottawa donna  all right heather euryx west in calgary  tonight thanks albertans also got a say  on whether to move to permanent daylight  saving time the change would eliminate  the need to change the clocks twice a  year province-wide results are due on  october 26th if the majority of people  vote yes the change will take effect  next fall and calgary voters were also  asked whether they want to reintroduce  fluoride to the city's drinking water it  has long been a contentious issue in  calgary this is the seventh time it's  been on the ballot yesterday a majority  of calgarians voted to bring it back  though the result is non-binding and the  new city council will make the final  decision  a little later i'll speak with the mayor  elect of calgary about why she thinks  she won and bridging divides in alberta  politics  in saskatchewan fewer than 300 new cases  of covid19 are being reported for the  second straight day that decline is good  news but it hasn't affected the  province's icus there are 83 patients  with covid19 in intensive care in  saskatchewan straining the system  patients needing critical care are now  being transferred to ontario pressure is  growing to reimpose public health  restrictions nathaniel dove reports  the fourth wave of covet 19 hit  saskatchewan hard  on monday the province began  transferring patients to ontario  infectious disease physician dr alex  wong says it was inevitable  we know informal triage is happening at  the bedside having to make hard  decisions again about who gets access to  you know sort of resources and who does  not after repeatedly setting daily case  count records new infections have  finally started to plateau but icu  admissions keep going up  wong says the province needs gathering  size restrictions on monday  saskatchewan's chief doctor agreed i  would certainly strongly recommend that  this is the time when we think of all  private gadgets in the home keep them to  a small consistent two or three  households only premier scott mo has  repeatedly said he won't impose new  restrictions city councilors in  saskatoon the provincial hot spot are  now exploring if they can create their  own the fact that we're the only  jurisdiction that doesn't have gathering  size restrictions even alberta does it  doesn't make sense to me  and things can still get worse the covet  19 viral load in saskatoon's wastewater  doubled since last week up 109 percent a  toxicologist says thanksgiving helped  the virus spread hospitalizations lag a  week to two weeks behind our numbers so  by the time people get sick end up sick  enough to be in the hospital and get  diagnosed takes some time saskatchewan  has finally asked for federal help  global news asked what exactly that  means in terms of new frontline staff  the government only said it takes a  strategic approach when it comes to  resource requests  wong says the fact that it comes weeks  after health care workers started  calling for aid shows politics are  guiding the province's pandemic response  if there's no further action then we're  just kind of going to see how it goes  we're going to be on our own  and facing a harsh reality that could  cost more lives  nathaniel dove global news saskatoon  there are lots of questions emerging  about covid19 vaccines for children  under the age of 12. pfizer has applied  to health canada to get them approved  for use the company says the formulation  for kids is the same as it is for adults  though kids would get a lower dose but  there is lots of other information  parents are still waiting for abigail  beeman reports  this far into the pandemic people are  exhausted  and the planners are exhausted and the  vaccinators are exhausted and so there's  not a lot of patience or tolerance for  delays and uncertainty and questions but  uncertainty and questions are exactly  what people planning for pediatric  vaccinations like kelly grindod are  facing late fall we're going to be doing  flu shots we're probably going to be  doing some third doses and high risk  groups and also kids vaccines so this is  all happening at once like the adult  vaccine how kids get the shot will be up  to each province or public health unit  we want to make sure it's equitable and  accessible to all children so it needs  to be in child friendly spaces while the  federal government has a contract with  pfizer that includes pediatric shots  ottawa is not yet saying how fast  they'll arrive if and when the under 12  pfizer vaccine gets the green light from  health canada the dosage for kids is one  third of the adult dose the biggest  question right now can kids be given a  smaller dosage of the same adult vaccine  drawing up pediatric doses from the  existing vials  is not something that is recommended at  this point  with for a number of reasons  we also understand from pfizer that  there's actual  the formulation has shifted but in a  statement to global news pfizer says the  formulation of the vaccine itself is the  same other than the dose being  administered based on the child's age  pfizer says it's working on a different  presentation 10 doses per vial a  different label and different color cap  for kids the public health agency of  canada did not offer clarification of dr  tam's comments by deadline it does make  for less precise dosing the primary  concern is are we able to precisely give  the amount of vaccine that's been  studied in pediatrics to kids if we're  using one third of the adult dose health  canada says it's making sure benefits of  the vaccine outweigh the risks for this  age group before any approval but isn't  offering a timeline abigail beeman  global news ottawa the rcmp is now  working with haitian and american police  to free a canadian who's one of 17  missionaries and their families  kidnapped over the weekend the rcmp is  not providing any more detail a  notorious haitian gang is demanding a 17  million dollar u.s ransom 1 million for  each hostage the missionaries were in  the country with the u.s based  non-profit group christian aid  ministries five children between the  ages of 8 months and 15 years are among  those abducted the identity of the  canadian has not been made public  former conservative prime minister  stephen harper on his way to saudi  arabia says he remains proud to have  negotiated a billion dollar deal to sell  the kingdom armored vehicles in a  statement posted on twitter harper says  he is also proud that this constructive  relationship helped secure jobs for  canadians through the largest export  manufacturing contract in canada's  history the 15 billion dollar deal was  signed in 2014 it involved manufacturing  hundreds of light armored vehicles  equipped with machine guns and cannon at  an ontario plant when justin trudeau was  elected he vowed he'd tried to cancel  the contract but later said the  cancellation fees were prohibitive human  rights groups have long opposed the sale  because of saudi arabia's poor human  rights record and warn it could use the  weapons against civilians harper who now  runs a consulting business will visit  saudi arabia almost three years to the  day after saudi agents murdered  washington post columnist jamal  khashoggi  kovid cases climb in the uk coming up  what's fueling the spike and why things  could get worse  plus have pot will travel one couple's  non-traditional way to get to their  wedding  in texas a close call on a runway this  small plane with 21 people on board was  attempting to take off but hit a fence  rolled off the runway and erupted in  flames everyone on board did manage to  escape safely only two people reported  minor injuries  there is pressure from some public  health doctors in the uk to reimpose  restrictions because cases of covet 19  are on the rise again there have been an  average of 43 000 new cases each day for  the last week the highest since  restrictions were lifted in july  hospital age hospitalizations and deaths  are also rising though they are nowhere  near the level at the start of the  pandemic  britain was the first western country to  roll out vaccines but that early success  could be part of the troubles today  redmond shannon explains  [Applause]  it's been exactly three months since  england left behind its last level of  lockdown no more vaccine passports  social distancing or mask mandates  as expected infections rose but with a  largely vaccinated population serious  cases and deaths stayed low but now  they're surging again a lot of the usual  symptoms and some of the odd ones which  i wasn't expecting canadian infectious  disease expert roland kayo is among the  increasing numbers of people in  isolation with covid19 here he says the  uk's early vaccine success is partly why  infections are now rising again so the  waning of vaccines is something we have  always expected okay this this is what  happens with any immune response you do  not keep it up forever all right the  question was how long it was and the  evidence over the past few months is  that it does decline and a substantial  people number of people have reduced  protection after about six months  diminishing effectiveness will also  depend on the type of vaccine the uk is  rolling out booster shots to the  vulnerable and healthcare workers  evidence from israel suggests that will  re-flatten the curve but britain only  recently started vaccinating under 18s  and that could be a problem at the  moment the epidemic is still being  driven in the uk by teenagers and  younger adults it's clear that the  vaccination program in 12 and 15 year  olds is  not going very well the worry is the  autumn winter is going to is going to  get very very messy the british  government says it is keeping a close  eye on the so-called delta plus variant  which experts say is slightly more  transmissible and resilient it says if  the pressure on the national health  service becomes too much this winter  then vaccine passports and masks may  return  donna all right redmond shannon in  london thank you  a couple in southern india didn't let  flash flooding ruin their wedding day  they found a way to adapt the bride and  groom sailed to the ceremony sitting in  this big metal cooking pot they floated  to the temple through muddy water two  men steering the way some members of the  family apparently swam other use others  used another pot to get there  the couple dry and hopefully living  happily ever after  meet calgary's new mayor ahead a  one-on-one with jody gondeck about her  historic election win  calgary made headlines in 2010 when it  elected the first muslim mayor of a  major north american city and now it's  done it again electing the first female  mayor in the city's history jyoti gandek  is of punjabi heritage and so is the new  mayor of edmonton amrajeet sohi if this  doesn't finally obliterate national  stereotypes of alberta as some kind of  racist backwater writes calgary herald  columnist dawn braid perhaps nothing  ever will well with me from calgary is  jody gondeck mayor-elect  first let me say congratulations to you  thank you so much  can you reflect a bit on your victory  what was it do you think about you and  your campaign the appeal to calgarians  well i think we offered people something  to vote for my campaign team and i have  been very clear from the beginning that  there must be a vision that's  communicated to calgarians and i think  the people of calgary embraced it they  wanted to hear more about what we would  do for resiliency in this city and they  demonstrated that they liked what they  heard by coming out to vote  you have a phd in urban sociology i've  read you've worked as an academic why  did you decide to make the leap to  public office and run for mayor did  something happen that made you say this  is it i'm running  you know it's been an interesting  journey falling in love with this  concept of city building  it started many years ago when i picked  up a couple of projects my dad had left  unfinished when he passed away quite  unfortunately in 2003.  and as i dug in to do some of that work  with his friends and colleagues i  realized that  there was a calling for me in public  service and i did a lot of volunteer  roles in and around the city and my  communities and in 2017 i looked back at  what i had been trying to do to  influence from the outside and i thought  maybe it was time to get a seat at the  table i felt well prepared at that time  and now with four years of counsel under  my belt i feel incredibly well prepared  to serve calgarians as their mayor  i know premier jason kenney has  congratulated you are you ideologically  aligned in any way and what sort of  relationship do you hope to have with  him  i think the greatest alignment that our  city and the provincial government could  have is one where we realize that we all  serve the same people so if we can count  on their assistance in providing the  supports that people need with public  health with child care access to housing  then we can help be that economic engine  again that carries us forward you know i  talked to nahad nenshi last month about  the toxic political atmosphere these  days how hostile and vicious some people  can be how much does that concern you  and and how do you think you'll push  back against it  it is quite concerning and i think a lot  of people who ran as candidates for the  first time in this election  realized just how ugly it can be but i  have a lot of faith that  we have a brighter future ahead i think  people are incredibly optimistic about  it and i think people have realized that  we need to band together to make sure  we're stamping out the voices that try  to be louder than ours  all right jyoti gondek mayor elect of  calgary thanks so much  thanks for having me on  up next rumblings from the red planet  what those sounds mean for exploration  on mars  a remarkable find off the coast of  israel a diver on a weekend treasure  hunt discovered this a sword experts  believe is roughly 900 years old dating  back to the crusades it's about a meter  long and was exposed when sands shifted  in the shallow water of a cove known for  sheltering ships the sword believed to  be made of iron will be x-rayed and  cleaned and maybe reveal clues about the  crusader who once carried it from an eye  on the distant past to an ear on the  distant present the first photograph  ever taken on the surface of mars was  beamed back to earth 45 years ago and  now sounds from the red planet captured  by nasa's perseverance rover are giving  scientists a deeper understanding of  what it's like to roam around up there  mike trolley explains  if your car made a racket like this  you'd get it checked out but nasa named  its mars rover perseverance not  rolls-royce  so all that clanking and scratching  they could care less yeah it's it's not  engineered for you know comfort  so there's definitely sounds of you know  moving up and down it's got a lot of  metal so there's going to be creaking  and groaning a little bit nasa has now  had a few months to study the hours of  sounds first recorded by the rover the  first audio ever captured on another  planet it's not as sexy as the lander's  descent or the subsequent drone flight  but hearing the winds of mars  adds to our sensory feel of another  world it doesn't really tie into the  search for life or anything like that  but it tells us that there were things  that we had predicted about the  atmosphere that we didn't quite get  right nasa scientists knew the martian  atmosphere was thin so they expected  high pitched sounds would either  disappear or appear muffled for example  this is what birds sound like on earth  and to be clear they haven't found birds  on mars but if they did this is what  you'd hear  what surprised them was how far sound  travels on the red planet the mars  helicopter was 80 meters away  yet the rover's microphones were still  able to pick it up proving science  doesn't just look cool with all of our  upcoming uh missions to you know  jupiter's moons different planets around  the solar system i think it would be a  nice um you know soundtrack you know  sounds of the solar system and here i  thought we already had that  [Music]  mike global news toronto  and that is global national for this  tuesday i'm donna friesen tonight's york  canada is near the village of packingham  ontario west of ottawa we'd love to see  your canada please email it to viewers  at globalnational.com  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  bye-bye  [Music]

